What is Spring Security ?
=====================
- Spring Security is the framewokr That protects the spring applications.
- It provides authentication, authorization, and many security features out of the box.

If you are trying to build a secure applications without spring security, you would need to manually handle…
	•	Core logic
		-	Write filters
		-	Handle sessions
		-	Create password validations
		-	Protects against common attacts
	•	Essentials Features
		-	Manage Login & Loguout
		-	Implement hashing 
		-	Configure CORS & CSRF
		-	Validate Requests

Main challenges
====================
* Authentication
	- You need to verify username/password yourself.
	- You must decide how to store and compare passwords securely.
	- Passwords should never be stored as plain text.
* Password security
	- You need proper hashing such as BCrypt/Argon2.
 	- You need to handle password changes, resets, failed login attempts, etc.
* Session management
	- After login, how do you remember that the user is authenticated?
	- You need to create, maintain, expire, and invalidate sessions securely.

* Authorization
	- Authentication answers “Who are you?”
	- Authorization answers “What are you allowed to do?”

For example:

	ADMIN → can delete users
	USER  → cannot delete users
	
•	Spring Security solves this problem by giving you a complete, well-tested security architecture. 
 It comes with:
	-	A powerful filter chain
	-	Built-in authentication mechanisms
	-	Simple ways to define user roles and permisions.
	
* Starts with Integrating Spring Security in Spring Boot App
================================================
1. Add Dependencies

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
</dependency>

 - Once you add the Spring Security dependency to your project, simply run the Spring Boot application.
 - Spring Security will automatically provide a default login form for your application.
 - To log in, use the default username and the password that Spring Security automatically generates. The generated password will be displayed in the **application console**.

 - Default Username: user
 - Generated Password: `5395812f-4398-4a15-82a0-ba1b10f41b3d`
 - Spring Security provides default /login  api  & /logout  api
 - Example: http://localhost:8080/api/login
 
 Different between Authentication & Authorization
 ========================================
 Authentication:
 	- Who are you
 	- It will ask identity
 	- Generally we are using for login
 	- Happens once at login
 Authorization:
 	- What you can do?
 	- It will verifies access (that means what you can access in applications)
	- Happens on every request.
	
Now, you may have a question:
===========================
 - Can we customize the default username and password provided by Spring Security?
 - Ans: Yes. Spring Boot provides properties that we can use to customize the default username and password.
 - When we add the Spring Security dependency and run the application, Spring Boot creates a default user. The default username is user, and a randomly generated password is displayed in the console.
 - The default security user configuration is represented by Spring Boot's SecurityProperties class. SecurityProperties is annotated with @ConfigurationProperties("spring.security").
    Inside SecurityProperties, there is a nested User class. The User class contains properties such as name and password. Because these properties are mapped using the 
    spring.security prefix, we can customize them in our application.properties file.

	For example:
	------------------
	- spring.security.user.name=sagar
    - spring.security.user.password=321

	Now, instead of the default username user, we can log in using:

	Username: sagar
	Password: 321
